@page "/"
@using Tedd.MOS65xx.Web.Components

<PageTitle>Tedd.MOS65xx - a cycle-exact Commodore 64 and 128 emulator in C#</PageTitle>

<section class="hero" id="top">
    <h1>Tedd.MOS65xx</h1>
    <p class="tagline">A cycle-exact Commodore 64 and Commodore 128 emulator in C#</p>
    <p class="lead">
        Every chip is clocked one system cycle at a time: no "run N cycles" shortcuts, no per-scanline
        approximations. The Commodore 128 adds the 8722 MMU, the Z80 that boots the machine and runs CP/M 3.0, the
        VIC-IIe 2 MHz mode and the 8563 VDC 80 column display. The same core drives the WPF desktop build, this
        browser build, SDL2 and Unity front-ends.
    </p>
    <p class="cta">
        <a class="btn btn-accent" href="#demo">Try it in your browser</a>
        <a class="btn" href="#download">Download for Windows</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/tedd/Tedd.MOS65xx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a>
    </p>
</section>

<section class="features" id="features">
    <h2>What is inside</h2>
    <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
            <h3>Cycle-stepped 6502</h3>
            <p>Every bus cycle of every opcode, documented and undocumented, verified against the 2.56 million
                <a href="https://github.com/SingleStepTests/65x02" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SingleStepTests</a> vectors
                and Klaus Dormann's functional test. Interrupt polling with the real SEI/CLI/branch delays.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <h3>VIC-II per cycle</h3>
            <p>PAL 6569 modelled after Christian Bauer's VIC-II article: bad lines, sprite DMA and priorities,
                all graphics modes including the invalid ones, border unit, raster interrupts, collisions,
                XSCROLL/YSCROLL - so raster tricks and demos behave.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <h3>CIA and SID</h3>
            <p>Two 6526 CIAs following Wolfgang Lorenz's software model (timer pipelines, TOD, serial
                shift register) and a reSID-derived 6581 with the exact envelope rate tables, combined waveforms,
                sync/ring modulation and the state-variable filter.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <h3>Real 1541 drive</h3>
            <p>A second cycle-stepped 6502 with two 6522 VIAs, the IEC bus with open-collector lines, spinning
                GCR disks (D64 converted to raw bit streams) with the four density zones, and byte-ready/SO timing.
                Fast loaders work because nothing is faked.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <h3>Commodore 128</h3>
            <p>The 8502 and a Zilog Z80 (every opcode, the undocumented flags, verified against 1.6 million
                SingleStepTests vectors) sharing one bus under the 8722 MMU: two RAM banks, common RAM, page 0/1
                relocation, C64 mode. The VIC-IIe's 2 MHz mode and extra keyboard lines, and the 8563 VDC with its own
                16K: 80 column text with attributes, 640 x 200 bitmap, block copy/fill. Boots BASIC 7 and CP/M 3.0
                from the included system disks.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <h3>Tools and tests</h3>
            <p>A two-pass 6502 assembler (all 256 opcodes), a PNG writer and an NUnit suite that boots the machine,
                compares VIC frames against reference images and runs assembled programs. The tech demo below is
                assembled at runtime by that assembler.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <h3>Many front-ends</h3>
            <p>The emulator core has no UI dependencies. A small hosting layer (session, key bindings, video/audio
                sinks) is shared by the WPF desktop app, this Blazor WebAssembly build, and SDL2 and Unity hosts.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

